# Single Cargo Target Directory (Disk First)

**User preference (mandatory for all AI agents in this repo): every Rust build and
test reuses the one shared `src-tauri/target/` directory. A second target
directory recompiles the whole Tauri dependency graph and wastes many GB of disk.**

## Forbidden

- `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<anything>` / `$env:CARGO_TARGET_DIR = ...`
- `cargo build --target-dir <dir>`, `cargo test --target-dir <dir>`,
  `cargo run --target-dir <dir>`, `cargo check --target-dir <dir>`
- Copying `target/debug` (or any part of `target/`) to make an "isolated"
  build or test sandbox
- Building inside a git worktree or scratch clone just to dodge a locked file
- Running a release build as a workaround for a locked debug binary (also
  forbidden by `.cursor/rules/no-release-builds.mdc`)

## When a build output is locked

`cargo test` / `cargo build` fails with
`failed to remove file ...\target\debug\app.exe` (or another `.exe`) when the
user's app or a CLI is still running.

1. Stop the build. Do not retry in another directory.
2. Tell the user which binary is locked and ask them to close it.
3. Re-run the same command in the shared `src-tauri/target/` after they confirm.

Never kill the user's running app or CLI process yourself.

## Allowed

- `cargo test`, `cargo test --test <name> <filter>`, `cargo build --bin <name>`,
  `cargo run --bin <name> -- ...` in `src-tauri/` with the default target dir
- Directories that IDE tooling creates by itself (`target/rust-analyzer`,
  `target/flycheck0`). Agents do not add new ones.

